WEBVTT

1
00:02:21.749 --> 00:02:26.666
<i>In 6th grade, Nora was told to write</i>
<i>an essay as if she was an object.</i>

2
00:02:26.791 --> 00:02:30.374
<i>She knew at once she'd be their house.</i>

3
00:02:34.583 --> 00:02:39.208
<i>She described how its belly shook</i>
<i>as she and her sister ran downstairs</i>

4
00:02:39.333 --> 00:02:41.416
<i>and out the back door.</i>

5
00:02:42.249 --> 00:02:47.708
<i>That it saw them take the shortcut</i>
<i>through the fence and onto the road,</i>

6
00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:51.541
<i>where the house no longer could see them.</i>

7
00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:59.374
<i>She wondered if the house liked</i>
<i>to be empty and light,</i>

8
00:03:00.124 --> 00:03:02.041
<i>or full and heavy.</i>

9
00:03:02.666 --> 00:03:05.791
<i>If the floors liked being trod on.</i>

10
00:03:06.833 --> 00:03:09.124
<i>If the walls were ticklish.</i>

11
00:03:09.249 --> 00:03:11.708
<i>If it ever felt pain.</i>

12
00:03:20.416 --> 00:03:24.624
<i>And she thought that yes,</i>
<i>it probably liked to be full.</i>

13
00:03:28.791 --> 00:03:31.833
<i>Before them, other people and pets</i>

14
00:03:31.958 --> 00:03:36.208
<i>had lived their moments in time there.</i>

15
00:03:36.333 --> 00:03:42.458
<i>Her great-great-grandfather died in</i>
<i>the room where her grandmother was born,</i>

16
00:03:42.583 --> 00:03:46.249
<i>which now was her parents' bedroom.</i>

17
00:03:51.791 --> 00:03:57.374
<i>Their father said the house had a flaw,</i>
<i>discovered right after it was finished</i>

18
00:03:57.499 --> 00:03:59.791
<i>a century ago.</i>

19
00:03:59.916 --> 00:04:05.541
<i>She wrote that it was as if the house</i>
<i>was sinking, collapsing,</i>

20
00:04:05.666 --> 00:04:11.291
<i>only in very slow motion,</i>
<i>and that her family's time there</i>

21
00:04:11.416 --> 00:04:15.874
<i>was just a split second in mid-air.</i>

22
00:04:23.833 --> 00:04:28.958
<i>When she read it later, she saw that</i>
<i>she hadn't used the word "quarrel",</i>

23
00:04:29.083 --> 00:04:32.416
<i>but wrote of the "noise" her parents made.</i>

24
00:04:32.541 --> 00:04:37.833
<i>But what the house disliked</i>
<i>more than noise, was silence.</i>

25
00:04:44.249 --> 00:04:47.708
<i>When her father left for good,</i>
<i>the house grew lighter.</i>

26
00:04:47.833 --> 00:04:51.374
<i>The noise her parents made, went away.</i>

27
00:04:51.499 --> 00:04:55.124
<i>But the house missed</i>
<i>the other sounds he made.</i>

28
00:05:01.166 --> 00:05:04.916
<i>Her teacher gave her an A,</i>
<i>and her father loved it.</i>

29
00:05:05.041 --> 00:05:10.749
<i>Nora reread it when looking for</i>
<i>a monologue for her academy audition,</i>

30
00:05:10.874 --> 00:05:14.124
<i>but found it disappointingly detached.</i>

31
00:05:14.249 --> 00:05:17.583
<i>She chose Nina's monologue</i>
<i>from "The Seagull" instead.</i>

32
00:05:17.708 --> 00:05:20.833
"I am a seagull. No. No.
I am an actress."

33
00:05:20.958 --> 00:05:23.041
"Yes, that is what I am."

34
00:05:23.583 --> 00:05:29.583
SENTIMENTAL VALUE

35
00:05:47.833 --> 00:05:49.916
<i>Nora?</i>

36
00:05:51.374 --> 00:05:54.333
<i>-Nora? Open the door.</i>
-I can't do it.

37
00:05:54.458 --> 00:05:57.291
<i>-Nora, open the door!</i>
-Just a moment.

38
00:06:16.708 --> 00:06:19.124
Open the door, please.

39
00:06:21.541 --> 00:06:23.624
Are you okay?

40
00:06:25.208 --> 00:06:28.499
I'm just going over my lines.

41
00:06:29.583 --> 00:06:31.999
But you know your lines.

42
00:06:34.291 --> 00:06:38.124
-We've been here before.
-No, it's not like that.

43
00:06:39.374 --> 00:06:42.249
I can't do it.
I can't go on stage.

44
00:06:42.374 --> 00:06:45.291
Okay. Let's take a deep breath.

45
00:06:49.416 --> 00:06:53.249
Just look at me. Look at me. Breathe.

46
00:06:54.541 --> 00:06:56.749
Okay. Just breathe.

47
00:06:57.874 --> 00:07:02.374
-I can't breathe in this thing.
-We'll fix it downstairs.

48
00:07:02.499 --> 00:07:04.458
Just come with me.

49
00:07:04.583 --> 00:07:08.083
-I need water.
-There's water downstairs.

50
00:07:10.083 --> 00:07:14.083
-It won't work. We're five minutes late.
-It'll be fine.

51
00:07:14.208 --> 00:07:16.791
Just get her on, okay?

52
00:07:31.666 --> 00:07:33.958
Oh, it's out of place.

53
00:07:34.083 --> 00:07:36.166
No, it's okay.

54
00:08:04.458 --> 00:08:07.291
Hey... Could you help me out?

55
00:08:07.416 --> 00:08:09.833
-Right backstage.
-Now?

56
00:08:17.333 --> 00:08:18.958
Just... a bit stressed.

57
00:08:19.083 --> 00:08:21.749
I think I need some...

58
00:08:21.874 --> 00:08:24.624
Sort of need... something.

59
00:08:28.083 --> 00:08:31.999
-No, there's no time. Stop it.
-We have time.

60
00:08:33.083 --> 00:08:36.166
-You're just...
-Okay, then hit me.

61
00:08:36.291 --> 00:08:39.041
-Just slap me.
-No!

62
00:08:39.166 --> 00:08:43.041
Otherwise I can't go on stage.
I can't go on stage.

63
00:08:45.416 --> 00:08:48.333
-Are you okay? Dammit.
-Yeah. Okay.

64
00:09:01.416 --> 00:09:02.583
Hey!

65
00:09:16.249 --> 00:09:18.541
-No!
-I can't breathe!

66
00:09:18.666 --> 00:09:20.666
Don't!

67
00:09:20.791 --> 00:09:22.999
Help! Nora, don't.

68
00:09:23.541 --> 00:09:26.666
-Don't... Don't tear it!
-Wait, Nora!

69
00:10:02.874 --> 00:10:05.541
Yngvar? Twenty seconds.

70
00:10:13.666 --> 00:10:15.749
Ready?

71
00:10:23.833 --> 00:10:25.666
Are we ready?

72
00:10:30.416 --> 00:10:32.499
Yes.

73
00:11:35.749 --> 00:11:37.666
Hear!

74
00:11:40.208 --> 00:11:42.874
You said you were going to save me.

75
00:11:42.999 --> 00:11:45.791
Yet you chased me into the flames!

76
00:12:01.666 --> 00:12:06.041
I let your children into my home.

77
00:12:06.166 --> 00:12:08.916
Still you bore witness against me!

78
00:12:58.791 --> 00:13:03.291
I told you we should get catering.
This is just stressful.

79
00:13:11.791 --> 00:13:14.083
Hey... Hey...

80
00:13:15.499 --> 00:13:18.041
I'm okay. I'm okay.

81
00:13:21.291 --> 00:13:23.583
-Mom? Can I have more cake?
-Sure.

82
00:13:23.708 --> 00:13:28.083
I've asked him to stop.
He's had four pieces already.

83
00:13:28.208 --> 00:13:32.041
-Please, mom?
-No, eat something else first.

84
00:13:32.166 --> 00:13:34.833
What a lovely speech you held.

85
00:13:34.958 --> 00:13:37.291
It was from both of us.

86
00:13:37.833 --> 00:13:43.333
Your mom was so proud of you,
she talked about it all the time.

87
00:13:43.458 --> 00:13:46.458
-Yeah.
-Well, Sissel... Jesus.

88
00:13:46.583 --> 00:13:50.333
She took me to
the National Theatre many times.

89
00:14:51.624 --> 00:14:56.374
You know, that was grandma's
office before she got sick.

90
00:14:57.124 --> 00:15:01.124
She sat there talking with people,
trying to help them.

91
00:15:01.249 --> 00:15:04.374
Or they spoke to her about their secrets.

92
00:15:11.666 --> 00:15:16.499
When I was little, I used to listen to
them talk through this stove.

93
00:15:19.124 --> 00:15:22.458
-Were you allowed to?
-No. They never knew.

94
00:15:24.624 --> 00:15:26.624
<i>It's been ages, but...</i>

95
00:15:26.749 --> 00:15:32.499
I can see it's hard to know what to do,
offer your condolences or congratulate.

96
00:15:32.624 --> 00:15:35.958
<i>Well, we spoke from time to time.</i>

97
00:15:38.541 --> 00:15:41.083
-What is it?
-Nothing.

98
00:15:47.041 --> 00:15:49.291
Dad's here.

99
00:15:49.416 --> 00:15:51.666
-Did you know?
-No.

100
00:15:51.791 --> 00:15:54.291
But I did inform him.

101
00:15:58.833 --> 00:16:01.291
Hi, dad!

102
00:16:01.416 --> 00:16:04.291
There you are! Excuse me.

103
00:16:05.374 --> 00:16:07.083
Hi.

104
00:16:07.208 --> 00:16:09.291
Hi. My girls.

105
00:16:11.874 --> 00:16:14.999
-Have you met Erik yet?
-No, is he here?

106
00:16:15.124 --> 00:16:20.916
-I didn't see you in church.
-I wasn't up for it. And why in church?

107
00:16:21.041 --> 00:16:25.083
-Did she find religion?
-We thought it would be nice.

108
00:16:25.958 --> 00:16:29.624
This is... This is so sad.

109
00:16:29.749 --> 00:16:34.458
She was a great mother for you, right?
She was so beautiful and...

110
00:16:34.583 --> 00:16:37.374
-Beautiful?
-Like you two.

111
00:16:37.499 --> 00:16:40.374
Sharp as hell.

112
00:16:40.499 --> 00:16:42.958
She was always right.

113
00:16:45.083 --> 00:16:48.499
-Hi! Look, grandpa's here.
-Well, hello!

114
00:16:48.624 --> 00:16:50.708
Huh? Good morning.

115
00:16:52.499 --> 00:16:55.124
-Yeah. Hi, hi.
-Hi. Been a while.

116
00:16:56.124 --> 00:16:59.624
-That's Even.
-Of course I know Evan.

117
00:17:00.708 --> 00:17:02.583
We go way back.

118
00:17:02.708 --> 00:17:05.958
My, how you've grown!

119
00:17:06.083 --> 00:17:10.499
-Are you the tallest boy in class?
-I don't know. Don't think so.

120
00:17:10.999 --> 00:17:13.124
Are you the class monster?

121
00:17:13.249 --> 00:17:14.791
No?

122
00:17:37.541 --> 00:17:39.541
Hi.

123
00:17:42.416 --> 00:17:44.499
Here.

124
00:17:50.624 --> 00:17:53.291
I was just thinking about you.

125
00:17:55.749 --> 00:17:58.583
-What's this?
-They're mine.

126
00:18:01.166 --> 00:18:03.291
Are you okay?

127
00:18:03.416 --> 00:18:05.541
Well, why do you ask?

128
00:18:05.666 --> 00:18:09.374
-We have to talk while I'm in town.
-Okay?

129
00:18:09.499 --> 00:18:12.291
There's something I need to tell you.

130
00:18:12.416 --> 00:18:16.708
-Can't you just tell me now?
-No, not here.

131
00:18:16.833 --> 00:18:21.041
I think it's time you and I sat down
and had a proper talk.

132
00:18:25.916 --> 00:18:28.958
Fredrik can take them in his Toyota.

133
00:18:29.083 --> 00:18:31.166
-Sorry.
-Oops.

134
00:18:36.791 --> 00:18:39.124
I'm good, thanks.

135
00:18:43.624 --> 00:18:47.083
I just hope he doesn't feel like he...

136
00:18:48.166 --> 00:18:50.791
Suddenly has to be a dad and...

137
00:18:52.374 --> 00:18:55.333
-Comfort me or something.
-No.

138
00:18:55.458 --> 00:18:58.208
But at least he's trying.

139
00:18:58.333 --> 00:19:01.416
It might not be easy for him either.

140
00:19:02.541 --> 00:19:05.583
Ask him what he plans to do
with the house.

141
00:19:05.708 --> 00:19:08.291
-What do you mean?
-He owns it.

142
00:19:11.791 --> 00:19:14.374
-Oh?
-Mom got it after the divorce.

143
00:19:14.499 --> 00:19:17.666
But they never filed the paperwork.

144
00:19:17.791 --> 00:19:19.999
Okay?

145
00:19:20.124 --> 00:19:24.333
It's unfortunate.
We were hoping for some money.

146
00:19:27.124 --> 00:19:29.833
Why don't you ask him?

147
00:19:29.958 --> 00:19:33.124
You never dare confront him
about anything.

148
00:19:33.249 --> 00:19:36.999
-I don't? I see.
-You don't.

149
00:19:39.333 --> 00:19:41.291
-Sure I do.
-No.

150
00:19:59.374 --> 00:20:00.708
-Hi.
-Hi.

151
00:20:02.749 --> 00:20:06.124
You look great.
What would you like?

152
00:20:06.499 --> 00:20:08.499
Uh, just a coffee.

153
00:20:08.624 --> 00:20:11.333
-Nothing else?
-No.

154
00:20:11.458 --> 00:20:15.458
-A coffee for the lovely young lady.
-Yes please.

155
00:20:15.583 --> 00:20:20.083
-And you? Did you enjoy your meal?
-I enjoy everything you serve.

156
00:20:25.541 --> 00:20:27.791
She thinks we're a couple.

157
00:20:29.916 --> 00:20:33.458
I've thought about you a lot.
I've been worried.

158
00:20:33.583 --> 00:20:36.749
Okay. How have you been?

159
00:20:36.874 --> 00:20:39.791
Well, fine. Got my hands full.

160
00:20:41.041 --> 00:20:46.083
You may have heard about the retrospective
of my films in France.

161
00:20:46.208 --> 00:20:51.624
-No, where should I have heard?
-The film I'm making is more important.

162
00:20:51.749 --> 00:20:54.166
You're hanging in there.

163
00:20:56.374 --> 00:20:59.666
It might be my best script yet.
Michael agrees.

164
00:20:59.791 --> 00:21:04.583
-So Michael is still producing?
-He says things are going my way.

165
00:21:04.708 --> 00:21:09.749
My documentary is being screened
in many countries. I'm on a roll.

166
00:21:16.833 --> 00:21:20.749
You had something you wanted
to discuss with me?

167
00:21:20.874 --> 00:21:23.249
Yes.

168
00:21:29.083 --> 00:21:31.416
The film is about a...

169
00:21:32.708 --> 00:21:35.541
A young woman, a mother who...

170
00:21:39.041 --> 00:21:41.749
Well, I want you in the lead role.

171
00:21:50.083 --> 00:21:52.499
I wrote it for you.

172
00:21:54.374 --> 00:21:57.374
You're the only one who can play it.

173
00:22:05.749 --> 00:22:07.166
Thank you.

174
00:22:11.666 --> 00:22:14.583
But you've never seen me act.

175
00:22:14.708 --> 00:22:17.833
Sure. Several times.
I saw you in "Medea".

176
00:22:17.958 --> 00:22:21.999
-You left during the intermission.
-I don't like theatre.

177
00:22:22.124 --> 00:22:26.249
The scenography was horrible.
But you were great.

178
00:22:27.708 --> 00:22:31.374
I can tell if an actor is any good
in two minutes.

179
00:22:31.499 --> 00:22:36.166
This part would be good for you
in many ways.

180
00:22:36.291 --> 00:22:40.583
You deserve something more personal
than centuries-old plays

181
00:22:40.708 --> 00:22:43.624
for a bunch of senior citizens.

182
00:22:43.749 --> 00:22:46.708
This is a real role in a film.

183
00:22:46.833 --> 00:22:52.458
Yes, you might get some financing too.
My series has done very well.

184
00:22:53.624 --> 00:22:56.583
Yeah, it helps somewhat.

185
00:22:56.708 --> 00:23:01.291
I've talked to Michael.
He's sure he can get financing.

186
00:23:01.416 --> 00:23:04.333
We'll film it in our house.

187
00:23:04.458 --> 00:23:06.666
-Our house?
-Yes.

188
00:23:06.791 --> 00:23:08.708
It's perfect.

189
00:23:17.249 --> 00:23:20.124
I'm not having any part in this.

190
00:23:21.124 --> 00:23:26.083
-You could take time off from the series.
-It was finished ages ago.

191
00:23:26.208 --> 00:23:29.166
You're way better than that.

192
00:23:29.291 --> 00:23:31.624
-You've seen it?
-Of course.

193
00:23:31.749 --> 00:23:33.999
Well, what do you think?

194
00:23:34.124 --> 00:23:36.666
What I think isn't important.

195
00:23:36.791 --> 00:23:39.791
I'd love to hear what you think.

196
00:23:41.041 --> 00:23:44.416
But that stuff isn't for me.
No visuals.

197
00:23:45.416 --> 00:23:49.916
We never really see your face.
We don't see your eyes.

198
00:23:50.041 --> 00:23:52.874
But what do I know?
People like it.

199
00:23:53.874 --> 00:23:57.499
It's not about what people think.
What do you think?

200
00:23:59.916 --> 00:24:02.458
You're not the problem with it.

201
00:24:03.291 --> 00:24:05.124
This part

202
00:24:05.249 --> 00:24:07.791
could be your big break.

203
00:24:08.874 --> 00:24:12.249
We're not going to work together, dad.

204
00:24:12.374 --> 00:24:16.583
-We can't even communicate.
-Just read it, and I'll call you.

205
00:24:16.708 --> 00:24:21.541
-Stop calling me. You're always drunk.
-But I worry about you.

206
00:24:21.666 --> 00:24:24.999
You don't have any right
to worry about me.

207
00:24:25.124 --> 00:24:27.958
-Just read the damn thing!
-Why?

208
00:24:28.083 --> 00:24:30.833
It'll never come to anything.

209
00:26:00.291 --> 00:26:02.374
Sit still!

210
00:26:02.499 --> 00:26:04.583
You mustn't move.

211
00:27:33.916 --> 00:27:37.958
I sat near you during the screening.
I looked at you,

212
00:27:38.083 --> 00:27:42.208
and I saw you were also very,
very moved by this screening.

213
00:27:45.583 --> 00:27:47.499
Uh, yes.

214
00:27:47.624 --> 00:27:50.124
I'm sorry. It's, oh...

215
00:27:51.208 --> 00:27:55.458
It's twenty years ago, probably,
since I saw it.

216
00:27:55.583 --> 00:28:01.083
And I remember vividly,
in detail, the time, and...

217
00:28:02.166 --> 00:28:05.999
The days on the set. The people.

218
00:28:06.124 --> 00:28:08.874
You know, I film with friends.

219
00:28:11.208 --> 00:28:13.458
They are my family.

220
00:28:14.916 --> 00:28:18.916
Yes, and...
Even more so in this movie, yes?

221
00:28:19.041 --> 00:28:22.833
You mean my daughter, Agnes? Yeah.

222
00:28:22.958 --> 00:28:25.583
She's the one that plays Anna.

223
00:28:28.874 --> 00:28:32.208
So, one last question before I let you go.

224
00:28:32.666 --> 00:28:37.416
I think everyone here wants to know.
There hasn't been a feature film

225
00:28:37.541 --> 00:28:40.583
since "L'Idylle" 15 years ago.

226
00:28:40.708 --> 00:28:44.458
Will we see another feature film
from Gustav Borg?

227
00:28:46.124 --> 00:28:48.874
Well, I hope so.

228
00:28:48.999 --> 00:28:52.208
I hope so too. Thank you, Mr. Borg.

229
00:29:20.208 --> 00:29:22.791
Oh, Gustav!

230
00:29:22.916 --> 00:29:24.958
Gustav!

231
00:29:25.083 --> 00:29:28.333
Sorry, sorry.
Rachel Kemp loved your film.

232
00:29:28.458 --> 00:29:32.999
She wants you to join her.
She's having dinner at L'Excelsior.

233
00:29:33.124 --> 00:29:35.124
You know?

234
00:29:35.249 --> 00:29:38.833
The festival is paying everything,
of course.

235
00:29:41.791 --> 00:29:47.499
I'm here with film director Gustav Borg.
He's joining Rachel Kemp's table.

236
00:29:54.958 --> 00:29:59.291
<i>-Excusez-moi</i> . It's a private event.
-Gustav Borg!

237
00:30:02.124 --> 00:30:05.708
-Thank you for coming!
-Let me get you a chair.

238
00:30:05.833 --> 00:30:08.166
-Here you go.
-Thank you.

239
00:30:10.083 --> 00:30:13.666
I was so impressed by your film.

240
00:30:13.791 --> 00:30:17.124
-It's a masterpiece. Really.
-Oh yeah.

241
00:30:17.666 --> 00:30:20.624
-Thank you.
-It is exceptional.

242
00:30:21.166 --> 00:30:24.499
-How did your screening go?
-It went well.

243
00:30:24.624 --> 00:30:28.374
The screening was amazing.
The theatre loved it.

244
00:30:28.499 --> 00:30:31.583
You have to see it in the fall
when it comes out.

245
00:30:41.041 --> 00:30:42.583
How does this work?

246
00:31:08.458 --> 00:31:11.374
I wish I could make films like you do.

247
00:31:18.083 --> 00:31:21.083
I just... It really struck me today.

248
00:31:24.083 --> 00:31:27.416
I don't know if I'd ever seen
a film like it before.

249
00:31:30.124 --> 00:31:32.166
It was really...

250
00:31:32.708 --> 00:31:34.916
Really...

251
00:31:35.041 --> 00:31:37.124
Moved me.

252
00:31:43.499 --> 00:31:44.958
Thank you.

253
00:31:53.291 --> 00:31:57.124
I've been thinking a lot about
taking a break, actually.

254
00:31:57.249 --> 00:32:00.624
I already have all these projects
lined up.

255
00:32:03.416 --> 00:32:06.749
They're good enough parts, but...

256
00:32:06.874 --> 00:32:11.333
-They don't have anything to do with me.
-Just say no.

257
00:32:12.791 --> 00:32:16.374
Sam would freak out
if I just walked away like that.

258
00:32:16.499 --> 00:32:18.458
He'd have a right to.

259
00:32:19.749 --> 00:32:21.874
Fuck Sam.

260
00:32:21.999 --> 00:32:24.416
It's your life.

261
00:32:27.041 --> 00:32:29.249
And you?

262
00:32:29.374 --> 00:32:32.208
You working on anything new?

263
00:32:32.333 --> 00:32:34.874
No. I thought I was, but...

264
00:32:34.999 --> 00:32:37.208
Couldn't get the money?

265
00:32:38.708 --> 00:32:40.791
Something like that.

266
00:33:18.458 --> 00:33:21.958
He'll take you to the hotel.

267
00:33:22.083 --> 00:33:24.166
Thank you.

268
00:33:28.249 --> 00:33:32.374
-It was very nice to meet you.
-Very nice.

269
00:33:32.499 --> 00:33:34.416
-Rach?
-Rachel!

270
00:33:34.541 --> 00:33:37.166
-Sir!
-No, no, no!

271
00:33:37.708 --> 00:33:40.874
Rach, I have your phone! Oh god!

272
00:34:23.916 --> 00:34:28.124
-Weren't you about to leave?
-You want me to leave? Okay.

273
00:34:28.249 --> 00:34:30.874
No, but this is getting very...

274
00:34:30.999 --> 00:34:33.583
Close and cuddly.

275
00:34:33.708 --> 00:34:38.874
-You don't like cuddly?
-Sure I like cuddly. I do.

276
00:34:38.999 --> 00:34:41.833
Why are you so afraid of intimacy?

277
00:34:44.791 --> 00:34:48.583
-I can tell that it isn't easy for you.
-No, it's not.

278
00:34:48.708 --> 00:34:53.083
It's perfect that you're married
so I don't have to confront

279
00:34:53.208 --> 00:34:56.041
how fucked-up I really am.

280
00:34:56.166 --> 00:35:01.333
Jeez, what a brutal thing to say
about yourself. How fucked-up are you?

281
00:35:04.916 --> 00:35:06.499
In percent?

282
00:35:06.624 --> 00:35:08.416
Eighty.

283
00:35:09.499 --> 00:35:13.166
-Eighty.
-So you're twenty percent...

284
00:35:13.291 --> 00:35:16.166
That's the percentage you see now.

285
00:35:19.333 --> 00:35:21.833
Did you talk to the theatre manager?

286
00:35:21.958 --> 00:35:24.583
He thinks I should

287
00:35:24.708 --> 00:35:26.791
see a therapist.

288
00:35:26.916 --> 00:35:28.374
Yes.

289
00:35:28.499 --> 00:35:31.208
-Well, why not?
-Don't say yes!

290
00:35:31.333 --> 00:35:36.333
No, but it's some sort of phobia.
Therapy might help.

291
00:35:36.458 --> 00:35:39.666
But it's not that bad, it was just that...

292
00:35:39.791 --> 00:35:43.499
-I had to hit you.
-Yeah. Yeah.

293
00:35:43.624 --> 00:35:47.208
Sorry about that.
It was pretty intense, but...

294
00:35:47.333 --> 00:35:50.874
-You could change your mind and report me.
-I won't.

295
00:35:50.999 --> 00:35:54.458
I swear.
I already know too much about myself.

296
00:35:54.583 --> 00:35:58.874
-I don't need therapy.
-I'd say the exact opposite.

297
00:35:58.999 --> 00:36:01.458
-Why not?
-Mom was a...

298
00:36:01.583 --> 00:36:05.874
-I mean, she was a therapist.
-You're not going to see your mom.

299
00:36:06.749 --> 00:36:10.166
-I just want one last kiss.
-Sure, but...

300
00:36:10.291 --> 00:36:13.041
-No, not like that.
-But we're done!

301
00:36:17.416 --> 00:36:19.791
-See you tomorrow.
-See you.

302
00:36:45.166 --> 00:36:49.249
I mean, when you go on stage,
it's so counterintuitive.

303
00:36:49.374 --> 00:36:54.208
Your body, your whole body screams
as you step out to face the audience.

304
00:36:54.333 --> 00:36:58.333
-There's nowhere to hide.
-But it's not all anxiety?

305
00:36:58.458 --> 00:37:00.541
No, I love it! I love it!

306
00:37:02.833 --> 00:37:05.874
And that...

307
00:37:05.999 --> 00:37:10.208
This frustration, anger,
the sense of injustice,

308
00:37:10.333 --> 00:37:12.833
the burden of responsibility.

309
00:37:12.958 --> 00:37:19.124
When she says he avoids conflicts,
she reveals what she hates about herself.

310
00:37:19.249 --> 00:37:22.749
What I like the most about it,
is probably...

311
00:37:22.874 --> 00:37:28.541
Building a character.
Diving into that perspective, or into

312
00:37:28.666 --> 00:37:32.916
their feelings by assuming
the role of someone else.

313
00:37:33.041 --> 00:37:38.333
It maybe provides me the security
to connect to my own feelings.

314
00:37:38.458 --> 00:37:41.374
So you don't want to be yourself?

315
00:37:41.499 --> 00:37:45.458
That's what it sounds like.
What would mom say about that?

316
00:37:52.458 --> 00:37:55.541
No, you're cheating. Step back. No.

317
00:37:55.666 --> 00:37:58.124
No, you can go closer.

318
00:37:58.249 --> 00:38:00.708
No, no. Here, here!

319
00:38:03.791 --> 00:38:06.208
Do you have a boyfriend?

320
00:38:10.124 --> 00:38:12.624
No, not really.

321
00:38:14.291 --> 00:38:16.374
Don't you want one?

322
00:38:18.958 --> 00:38:21.541
Sure.

323
00:38:21.666 --> 00:38:24.416
But it's not always that easy.

324
00:38:27.291 --> 00:38:29.708
-But I love you.
-Yes.

325
00:38:31.416 --> 00:38:33.499
I love you too.

326
00:38:35.083 --> 00:38:37.958
I wanna marry you when I grow up.

327
00:38:42.999 --> 00:38:45.124
It's nice of you to say so.

328
00:38:45.249 --> 00:38:47.874
-But it's impossible.
-I know.

329
00:38:53.624 --> 00:38:57.083
-Did you fall asleep?
-No.

330
00:38:57.208 --> 00:38:59.374
He's just so cute!

331
00:39:01.249 --> 00:39:04.874
I think... He proposed to me down there.

332
00:39:06.583 --> 00:39:09.999
-Congrats.
-That doesn't happen every day.

333
00:39:11.208 --> 00:39:15.291
When he returned from
the playground today, he came to me,

334
00:39:15.416 --> 00:39:18.916
looked straight at me and said:
"I see you."

335
00:39:20.708 --> 00:39:22.458
Where's that from?

336
00:39:22.583 --> 00:39:26.583
-Do you say that?
-Maybe he's picked it up at school.

337
00:39:26.708 --> 00:39:29.541
Saying "I see you" at school?

338
00:39:29.666 --> 00:39:31.749
Are you okay?

339
00:39:32.833 --> 00:39:35.041
-Sorry.
-Hey?

340
00:39:35.166 --> 00:39:37.333
-What is it?
-I'm okay.

341
00:39:37.458 --> 00:39:40.291
I think I should head home.

342
00:39:40.416 --> 00:39:42.041
No, wait.

343
00:39:42.166 --> 00:39:44.583
-I'm okay.
-Are you sure?

344
00:39:46.458 --> 00:39:50.583
-Really? Nothing's happened?
-No. It's okay. Sorry.

345
00:39:53.624 --> 00:39:55.291
Okay.

346
00:40:11.874 --> 00:40:13.958
-Is she alright?
-Yeah.

347
00:40:18.833 --> 00:40:23.249
-You don't think... She's not there again?
-I don't think so.

348
00:40:25.083 --> 00:40:27.499
I think she's just lonely.

349
00:40:29.583 --> 00:40:33.541
-Does that make you sad?
-A bit.

350
00:40:56.583 --> 00:40:58.666
-Hi.
-Hi.

351
00:41:31.499 --> 00:41:33.208
It's weird.

352
00:41:34.541 --> 00:41:37.791
When mom fell ill,
I considered whether to

353
00:41:37.916 --> 00:41:41.416
take over the house,
live here with Even and Erik.

354
00:41:42.041 --> 00:41:44.958
-Would you want to live here?
-No.

355
00:41:45.083 --> 00:41:47.916
We can't afford to buy out anyone.

356
00:41:56.333 --> 00:42:00.708
I've been through all that,
it's just old documents.

357
00:42:01.999 --> 00:42:05.583
Well, thanks for everything you've done.

358
00:42:05.708 --> 00:42:07.708
It's amazing.

359
00:42:08.708 --> 00:42:14.249
-Keep what you want and dump the rest.
-But there are many nice things here.

360
00:42:14.374 --> 00:42:17.833
Things with sentimental value.
Have a look.

361
00:42:18.916 --> 00:42:21.541
I'd like that vase, for example.

362
00:42:22.458 --> 00:42:25.208
-Yeah, it's kinda nice.
-Oh?

363
00:42:25.333 --> 00:42:28.499
-Because I want it?
-No. You can have it.

364
00:42:28.624 --> 00:42:32.958
No, the point is to go through it together
and sort it out.

365
00:42:33.083 --> 00:42:35.916
Yeah.
Well, then I'll take it.

366
00:42:37.291 --> 00:42:39.958
My god.

367
00:42:44.208 --> 00:42:46.708
Remember to turn off the stove

368
00:42:59.499 --> 00:43:04.083
-How long is he going to stay here?
-He didn't say.

369
00:43:04.208 --> 00:43:07.583
But he's here to arrange the sale, right?

370
00:43:07.708 --> 00:43:10.208
I don't really know if...

371
00:43:10.333 --> 00:43:13.541
He'll be here around 2 p.m.
You can ask him.

372
00:43:14.874 --> 00:43:18.333
-I don't think so.
-You can't pretend he's not here.

373
00:43:18.458 --> 00:43:20.749
I can try.

374
00:43:23.833 --> 00:43:27.499
He isn't selling the house,
like I thought, but...

375
00:43:28.583 --> 00:43:31.208
Well, he has this project.

376
00:43:32.666 --> 00:43:34.666
Come again?

377
00:43:34.791 --> 00:43:38.124
It's a good thing.
He's making a new movie.

378
00:43:40.041 --> 00:43:42.958
What project?

379
00:43:43.083 --> 00:43:46.833
-The one you discussed.
-The film he wrote for me?

380
00:43:46.958 --> 00:43:49.458
Yeah, the part you didn't want.

381
00:43:51.124 --> 00:43:54.624
-Did he get financing?
-He must have.

382
00:43:54.749 --> 00:43:58.916
He didn't say much.
But he asked if you'd be here.

383
00:44:00.249 --> 00:44:02.208
Okay.

384
00:44:05.583 --> 00:44:08.374
-Is that him?
-Yeah.

385
00:44:26.499 --> 00:44:28.333
Shit, that's Rachel Kemp.

386
00:44:30.166 --> 00:44:32.083
Oh, shit.

387
00:44:40.083 --> 00:44:42.958
-Shit!
-Hey? Nora?

388
00:44:43.083 --> 00:44:45.166
Hey, now...

389
00:44:45.833 --> 00:44:49.999
Looks almost the same.
Nothing has really changed.

390
00:45:07.666 --> 00:45:09.833
It's so great to see you again.

391
00:45:09.958 --> 00:45:14.291
There's a café nearby. Rachel will
let you know when we're done.

392
00:45:23.249 --> 00:45:25.958
Hi. Is Nora here?

393
00:45:26.083 --> 00:45:28.166
No, she had to leave.

394
00:45:29.624 --> 00:45:33.416
-Rachel, this is my daughter, Agnes.
-Hi. Rachel.

395
00:45:33.541 --> 00:45:35.666
Nice to meet you.

396
00:45:36.833 --> 00:45:41.791
-Are you in the business as well?
-No, she helps me with research sometimes.

397
00:45:41.916 --> 00:45:45.374
I'm an historic...
Academic historian. Yeah.

398
00:45:45.499 --> 00:45:48.416
Sorry, I can't even speak properly.

399
00:45:50.374 --> 00:45:53.208
Do you recognise her?

400
00:45:54.416 --> 00:45:57.791
-She played Anna in the film you saw.
-A long time ago.

401
00:45:59.458 --> 00:46:02.041
Of course. That's...

402
00:46:02.166 --> 00:46:05.374
You're Anna! That's right! I see it.

403
00:46:05.499 --> 00:46:09.541
-Can I take a selfie with you?
-Yeah, sure.

404
00:46:09.666 --> 00:46:11.749
Starstruck.

405
00:46:19.291 --> 00:46:20.374
Thank you.

406
00:46:21.833 --> 00:46:25.249
So she's here, talking to her son.

407
00:46:25.374 --> 00:46:29.833
This is the last scene.
She's preparing him something to eat.

408
00:46:29.958 --> 00:46:34.083
It's important for her.
A last motherly touch, maybe.

409
00:46:35.833 --> 00:46:37.999
They hug, and he leaves.

410
00:46:39.083 --> 00:46:43.291
And the moment the door closes,
something in her changes.

411
00:46:43.416 --> 00:46:46.833
Almost like she relaxes, don't you think?

412
00:46:48.583 --> 00:46:52.958
The boy's meeting up with his father,
so she's not in a hurry.

413
00:46:53.083 --> 00:46:58.458
-And here's the wire to dry clothes on.
-A clothes line.

414
00:46:58.583 --> 00:47:01.624
-A clothing line.
-The clothing line.

415
00:47:01.749 --> 00:47:05.374
And then she goes in here
and starts preparing.

416
00:47:11.166 --> 00:47:13.249
Suddenly...

417
00:47:14.333 --> 00:47:17.749
She hears the veranda door opening.
Come.

418
00:47:24.166 --> 00:47:28.583
It's her son. He's forgotten his flag.
His Norwegian flag.

419
00:47:28.708 --> 00:47:33.541
Yeah, it's the 17th of May.
It's like the national day.

420
00:47:33.666 --> 00:47:37.166
Did I say that all this will be
in one shot?

421
00:47:37.291 --> 00:47:39.999
A oner? This whole thing?

422
00:47:40.124 --> 00:47:43.499
The whole ending in just one shot.
No editing.

423
00:47:43.624 --> 00:47:47.374
-Complete sync between time and place.
-Wow.

424
00:47:48.458 --> 00:47:50.458
So...

425
00:47:50.583 --> 00:47:54.291
The boy just stands there looking at her.

426
00:47:54.416 --> 00:47:57.333
Something's off. He doesn't get it.

427
00:48:00.208 --> 00:48:03.874
So he just grabs his flag and runs off.

428
00:48:06.624 --> 00:48:11.583
She goes here to make sure that
this time he really leaves.

429
00:48:11.708 --> 00:48:17.999
We only hear the gate closing behind him.
Because the camera's here, on her.

430
00:48:18.124 --> 00:48:20.249
And this is crucial.

431
00:48:21.333 --> 00:48:23.916
The expression she has here.

432
00:48:25.749 --> 00:48:27.833
What is she thinking?

433
00:48:27.958 --> 00:48:30.041
Exactly.

434
00:48:32.374 --> 00:48:34.458
Then she walks back here.

435
00:48:54.124 --> 00:48:57.083
-So this is where she does it?
-Yeah.

436
00:49:05.916 --> 00:49:08.749
You think that would bear her weight?

437
00:49:09.749 --> 00:49:13.041
Well, it happened like that.
With my mother.

438
00:49:14.124 --> 00:49:16.416
-Your mother?
-Yeah.

439
00:49:22.374 --> 00:49:24.666
-But not here?
-Yeah.

440
00:49:25.708 --> 00:49:29.291
-It's a very long time ago.
-No!

441
00:49:29.749 --> 00:49:30.874
She used that.

442
00:49:34.041 --> 00:49:37.083
-That?
-Uh-huh.

443
00:49:37.208 --> 00:49:39.958
In order to get high enough.

444
00:49:41.041 --> 00:49:43.333
But we won't see that.

445
00:49:43.458 --> 00:49:47.916
We'll end the shot with her going back in
and closing the door.

446
00:49:48.041 --> 00:49:50.708
We just hear the sound.

447
00:49:51.749 --> 00:49:54.791
As the camera moves away.

448
00:49:59.708 --> 00:50:03.541
Can I ask
why you didn't just leave after that?

449
00:50:03.666 --> 00:50:06.166
Well, we did leave after this.

450
00:50:06.291 --> 00:50:09.541
We moved to Sweden.
My father was Swedish.

451
00:50:11.416 --> 00:50:15.124
Well, I'd love to hear more about her.

452
00:50:16.291 --> 00:50:19.083
It's not my mother in the script,
you know.

453
00:50:24.374 --> 00:50:27.708
Right, but they would still let me do it?
Yeah.

454
00:50:27.833 --> 00:50:31.249
-Yeah, I know.
-This'll be great.

455
00:50:31.374 --> 00:50:35.958
She's motivated.
I told her that that footstool

456
00:50:36.083 --> 00:50:39.166
was the one my mom used to hang herself.

457
00:50:39.291 --> 00:50:41.541
The one from IKEA?

458
00:50:58.249 --> 00:51:00.333
"Is this me?"

459
00:51:02.249 --> 00:51:07.083
"Is this me?" What I think,
the way I feel, how I sleep or not...

460
00:51:07.208 --> 00:51:10.416
I just can't understand how this...

461
00:51:10.541 --> 00:51:13.666
-Can be me.
-We've been here before.

462
00:51:14.749 --> 00:51:18.249
<i>-So you don't recognise yourself?</i>
<i>-No.</i>

463
00:51:19.624 --> 00:51:25.041
<i>Many years earlier, the therapist's office</i>
<i>was called "The Library".</i>

464
00:51:28.791 --> 00:51:33.041
<i>It was often empty.</i>
<i>Karin, the oldest daughter,</i>

465
00:51:33.166 --> 00:51:36.958
<i>hung out with friends</i>
<i>and listened to music there.</i>

466
00:52:04.208 --> 00:52:08.624
<i>When the war broke out,</i>
<i>Karin joined the resistance.</i>

467
00:52:09.416 --> 00:52:14.416
<i>One autumn evening in 1943,</i>
<i>the State Police came to fetch her.</i>

468
00:52:21.833 --> 00:52:27.791
<i>She was imprisoned for two years.</i>
<i>She never talked about the prison camp.</i>

469
00:52:35.541 --> 00:52:37.208
There. Listen to this.

470
00:52:38.541 --> 00:52:44.083
<i>After the war, Karin got married</i>
<i>and took over the family house.</i>

471
00:52:46.083 --> 00:52:49.708
<i>She gave birth to Gustav in 1951.</i>

472
00:52:56.333 --> 00:53:01.041
<i>Many years later, when he had become</i>
<i>a filmmaker, Gustav liked to say:</i>

473
00:53:01.166 --> 00:53:04.708
<i>"Nothing is more beautiful than shadows."</i>

474
00:53:04.833 --> 00:53:08.833
<i>He would imagine his childhood home.</i>

475
00:53:52.624 --> 00:53:58.374
<i>Karin's little sister, Edith, took over</i>
<i>the house with her friend Lillian.</i>

476
00:53:58.499 --> 00:54:03.749
<i>Edith was happily childless,</i>
<i>but Gustav often visited them in summer.</i>

477
00:54:10.458 --> 00:54:14.833
<i>If anyone complained, Edith turned up</i>
<i>the volume. She was sure</i>

478
00:54:14.958 --> 00:54:19.374
<i>one of the neighbours</i>
<i>had ratted on her sister during the war.</i>

479
00:54:28.166 --> 00:54:32.624
<i>When Edith died,</i>
<i>Gustav was her sole heir.</i>

480
00:54:32.749 --> 00:54:37.833
<i>In 1986, he moved back to the house</i>
<i>with all its memories.</i>

481
00:54:41.541 --> 00:54:46.208
<i>There are two versions of</i>
<i>how Gustav met his wife, Sissel.</i>

482
00:54:46.333 --> 00:54:50.333
<i>She said he came to her as</i>
<i>a patient with insomnia.</i>

483
00:54:50.458 --> 00:54:54.333
<i>It was obvious to her that moving back</i>
<i>proved more complicated</i>

484
00:54:54.458 --> 00:54:56.374
<i>than he liked to admit.</i>

485
00:54:56.916 --> 00:54:59.499
<i>Gustav's take was grander.</i>

486
00:54:59.624 --> 00:55:01.708
She was absolutely stunning.

487
00:55:01.833 --> 00:55:06.666
I found out she was a therapist,
so I got some mental problems.

488
00:55:06.791 --> 00:55:09.999
<i>Six months later, Sissel was pregnant.</i>

489
00:55:11.041 --> 00:55:16.083
<i>They were married three weeks before</i>
<i>Nora was born in December 1987.</i>

490
00:55:18.333 --> 00:55:22.708
<i>With her little sister Agnes,</i>
<i>they became a family of four.</i>

491
00:55:24.874 --> 00:55:29.999
-So it sucks? Being with us sucks?
-That's not what I'm saying!

492
00:55:30.124 --> 00:55:34.374
Why do you have to dredge up
that old crap again?

493
00:55:35.708 --> 00:55:39.166
<i>After the divorce,</i>
<i>Gustav returned to Sweden.</i>

494
00:55:40.249 --> 00:55:43.541
<i>The house grew lighter and lighter.</i>

495
00:56:06.458 --> 00:56:08.624
Have you talked to Peter?

496
00:56:09.708 --> 00:56:12.458
Peter is an old man.

497
00:56:13.583 --> 00:56:17.416
-He hasn't shot a film in ten years.
-Neither have I.

498
00:56:18.499 --> 00:56:23.624
-No, but he's retired.
-He'll do it for me. I'll talk to him.

499
00:56:26.458 --> 00:56:29.833
-I won't let anyone else film it.
-Okay, but...

500
00:56:29.958 --> 00:56:34.041
-You have to go and speak to him yourself.
-Sure thing.

501
00:56:34.166 --> 00:56:36.624
"Sure thing"? Just do it.

502
00:56:38.249 --> 00:56:42.166
-He's no older than you are.
-Blah, blah, blah.

503
00:56:42.291 --> 00:56:46.083
-So how do you like Norway?
-Oh, I love Norway.

504
00:56:46.208 --> 00:56:48.041
It's beautiful.

505
00:56:48.166 --> 00:56:51.291
The people are very friendly.

506
00:56:51.416 --> 00:56:53.749
Been very welcoming.

507
00:56:55.166 --> 00:57:00.374
This is a Netflix production.
Will it be shown in cinemas?

508
00:57:00.499 --> 00:57:02.666
Sure, where else?

509
00:57:02.791 --> 00:57:07.666
It's still being negotiated,
but that's definitely the plan.

510
00:57:09.458 --> 00:57:11.874
Another question for Rachel Kemp.

511
00:57:11.999 --> 00:57:16.374
It's about the reception of
your last film, "Cathy's Choice".

512
00:57:16.499 --> 00:57:20.083
It must have made you a bit disappointed.

513
00:57:22.458 --> 00:57:25.208
I'm... I'm very proud of that film.

514
00:57:26.458 --> 00:57:30.583
I don't think that's what
we're here for today, are we?

515
00:57:30.708 --> 00:57:33.874
Were you nervous before taking on, like,

516
00:57:33.999 --> 00:57:36.166
a real drama film?

517
00:57:36.291 --> 00:57:39.124
I'm working...
I'm doing this film because...

518
00:57:39.249 --> 00:57:42.291
What sort of a stupid question is that?

519
00:57:43.458 --> 00:57:47.749
Rachel Kemp is the best actress of her
generation. You're lucky she's here.

520
00:57:47.874 --> 00:57:50.624
You should be grateful!

521
00:57:50.749 --> 00:57:53.083
Get out!

522
00:57:53.208 --> 00:57:57.208
-Get out!
-Okay. Thank you.

523
00:57:59.249 --> 00:58:01.833
What a goddamn idiot.

524
00:58:01.958 --> 00:58:05.416
Shouldn't the questions
be clarified in advance?

525
00:58:05.541 --> 00:58:09.458
-That's not possible.
-The TikTok troll.

526
00:58:31.541 --> 00:58:35.583
Surely not ASMR again?
It makes you fall asleep.

527
00:58:35.708 --> 00:58:39.666
-He taught it to me.
-I also did it with him.

528
00:58:39.791 --> 00:58:42.541
I won't hold a speech, but I feel

529
00:58:42.666 --> 00:58:46.958
this gratitude welling up in me.

530
00:58:47.083 --> 00:58:51.541
I just want to say how awesome it is
to work with every one of you.

531
00:58:51.666 --> 00:58:56.499
-Of course there's still some way to go...
-We talked about it earlier.

532
00:58:56.624 --> 00:58:59.249
We've also talked a great deal.

533
00:58:59.374 --> 00:59:02.208
We'll sort it out before the premiere.

534
00:59:02.333 --> 00:59:06.124
And Jakob, I know things
are difficult on the home front.

535
00:59:07.583 --> 00:59:10.166
But... Uh, but...

536
00:59:10.291 --> 00:59:15.374
Thank you for doing the work you do,
and if there's anything, tell me.

537
00:59:15.499 --> 00:59:18.708
-Always.
-Thank you. I will.

538
00:59:18.833 --> 00:59:20.791
But... Okay.

539
00:59:20.916 --> 00:59:23.958
But I think this is gonna be awesome.

540
00:59:24.083 --> 00:59:26.208
-I think so too.
-Cheers!

541
00:59:26.333 --> 00:59:29.166
-Cheers!
-Cheers!

542
00:59:29.708 --> 00:59:33.416
But you haven't told me
you're getting a divorce.

543
00:59:33.541 --> 00:59:37.708
No, it's been so busy with
the rehearsals, and... Well.

544
00:59:37.833 --> 00:59:39.833
Yeah.

545
00:59:41.791 --> 00:59:45.833
-It's been hard to find the right moment.
-Of course.

546
00:59:48.208 --> 00:59:50.291
It's not like...

547
00:59:52.583 --> 00:59:55.749
-Not because of us?
-No, that's not it.

548
01:00:00.374 --> 01:00:02.458
I'm here for you, if...

549
01:00:03.583 --> 01:00:05.541
If you need to talk.

550
01:00:05.666 --> 01:00:08.999
<i>-Is nobody else taking a dip?</i>
<i>-Get in!</i>

551
01:00:09.124 --> 01:00:11.124
<i>-One, two...</i>
<i>-Okay.</i>

552
01:00:11.249 --> 01:00:13.791
-Three!
-Whoo!

553
01:00:25.666 --> 01:00:27.874
We don't need to hide now.

554
01:00:30.083 --> 01:00:33.499
Listen, I... I don't know.

555
01:01:09.583 --> 01:01:11.666
-Hello!
-Hi!

556
01:01:13.791 --> 01:01:16.124
-Is dad here?
-Not yet.

557
01:01:19.166 --> 01:01:22.749
-There you are! Congratulations!
-Thanks.

558
01:01:23.499 --> 01:01:26.791
Oh, wow! You look nice with a bowtie!

559
01:01:26.916 --> 01:01:29.083
Hello! Are we having pizza?

560
01:01:29.208 --> 01:01:32.041
Hello. I'll just do like this.

561
01:01:36.874 --> 01:01:39.333
That record's still mine.

562
01:01:39.458 --> 01:01:41.916
Nah.

563
01:01:42.041 --> 01:01:44.833
Start there.

564
01:01:51.249 --> 01:01:53.958
Hi! Welcome.

565
01:01:54.083 --> 01:01:56.999
-Good to see you.
-Likewise.

566
01:01:57.124 --> 01:02:00.291
Well, congratulations. Happy birthday!

567
01:02:00.416 --> 01:02:04.083
The nine year old man. Big boy now.

568
01:02:04.208 --> 01:02:10.208
-Guess you haven't met since our wedding.
-I can never forget a woman like Therese.

569
01:02:14.833 --> 01:02:19.708
-Hi. Wow! How nice. Where did you get it?
-Just get some glasses.

570
01:02:32.791 --> 01:02:34.874
Hi, dad.

571
01:02:36.374 --> 01:02:38.458
Hi.

572
01:02:43.583 --> 01:02:45.499
Yeah, you hit it.

573
01:02:50.666 --> 01:02:53.166
How's the film coming along?

574
01:02:55.749 --> 01:02:58.833
-Good.
-I heard of it. How exciting!

575
01:02:59.374 --> 01:03:03.124
-What's it like to work with Rachel Kemp?
-Hey...

576
01:03:04.999 --> 01:03:09.083
I'm just asking what it's like
to work with her.

577
01:03:09.208 --> 01:03:12.249
Well, she's not there yet. But almost.

578
01:03:12.374 --> 01:03:16.916
I heard you fooled her with
a footstool from IKEA.

579
01:03:17.041 --> 01:03:20.499
Is he gonna spend all day
watching that thing?

580
01:03:20.624 --> 01:03:23.416
He's got 20 minutes screen time left.

581
01:03:23.541 --> 01:03:25.541
Who? Even?

582
01:03:29.416 --> 01:03:34.291
I just wanna know how much screen time
you've left. Aren't you over now?

583
01:03:34.416 --> 01:03:38.374
-How much screen time I've left?
-Does he have any?

584
01:03:38.499 --> 01:03:40.874
-Put it away.
-The pizza's ready.

585
01:03:40.999 --> 01:03:43.291
-I just...
-Right now.

586
01:03:55.791 --> 01:03:58.791
-Right. What do you say?
-Thank you.

587
01:03:58.916 --> 01:04:01.916
Look here. Monica Bellucci, dammit.

588
01:04:03.958 --> 01:04:08.583
And this film will make you understand
women and maternal relationships.

589
01:04:08.708 --> 01:04:10.458
How nice.

590
01:04:10.583 --> 01:04:13.499
-Thanks, but we don't have a DVD player.
-Thank god.

591
01:04:13.624 --> 01:04:17.666
-We'll find a way to see them.
-I rarely watch films anymore.

592
01:04:51.958 --> 01:04:55.041
You should start writing.

593
01:04:55.166 --> 01:04:57.333
Yeah, so you say.

594
01:04:58.624 --> 01:05:02.999
Why wait for some old fart
to let you play Ophelia?

595
01:05:03.124 --> 01:05:07.249
-I'm playing Hamlet.
-I talked to Rachel about it.

596
01:05:07.374 --> 01:05:12.499
She's started developing her own projects.
Has a production company now.

597
01:05:12.624 --> 01:05:14.708
It's hard to be an actress.

598
01:05:14.833 --> 01:05:19.458
You decide nothing yourself.
Just wait for people to want you.

599
01:05:19.583 --> 01:05:22.874
-It's not healthy in the long run.
-I...

600
01:05:22.999 --> 01:05:29.541
It erodes your self-confidence, and your
need for attention becomes insufferable.

601
01:05:32.124 --> 01:05:35.041
I could never have married an actress.

602
01:05:35.583 --> 01:05:37.791
But fucking them was okay?

603
01:05:40.541 --> 01:05:45.999
Artists today are so petite bourgeoisie.
You'll never write "Ulysses"

604
01:05:46.124 --> 01:05:49.583
driving to soccer practice
and comparing car insurance.

605
01:05:51.916 --> 01:05:56.291
What happened to artistic freedom?
Artists need liberty.

606
01:05:58.499 --> 01:06:01.124
And not have kids then, dad?

607
01:06:03.958 --> 01:06:06.749
Don't say that, Nora.

608
01:06:06.874 --> 01:06:08.958
How old are you now?

609
01:06:10.333 --> 01:06:13.916
When Sissel was your age,
she had two children.

610
01:06:14.041 --> 01:06:16.083
Times have changed.

611
01:06:18.916 --> 01:06:21.916
You two are the best thing
that's happened to me.

612
01:06:22.041 --> 01:06:24.583
The best that's happened to you?

613
01:06:24.708 --> 01:06:26.791
Then why weren't you there?

614
01:06:29.249 --> 01:06:31.833
Everyone's mad at dad, huh?

615
01:06:33.708 --> 01:06:36.624
You two turned out fine, didn't you?

616
01:06:36.749 --> 01:06:40.958
How can you tell?
You don't even know us.

617
01:06:41.083 --> 01:06:44.583
-Stop exaggerating.
-Exaggerating?

618
01:06:50.624 --> 01:06:54.708
I know it's been hard for you.
I recognize myself in you.

619
01:06:57.874 --> 01:07:00.791
But you're so goddamn angry.

620
01:07:00.916 --> 01:07:04.958
It's hard to love someone
who's so full of rage.

621
01:07:05.624 --> 01:07:09.208
Right?
You don't want to be alone forever.

622
01:07:13.708 --> 01:07:16.749
It's not good for the art either.

623
01:07:16.874 --> 01:07:21.041
You need someone to care about.
Children or something.

624
01:07:21.708 --> 01:07:24.458
You never regret having children.

625
01:09:58.624 --> 01:10:01.249
Peter?

626
01:10:02.791 --> 01:10:05.041
Good to see you. Hi.

627
01:10:05.166 --> 01:10:07.583
Some place you have here.

628
01:10:07.708 --> 01:10:11.874
Yeah, thanks to Lasse Hallström, not you.

629
01:10:12.416 --> 01:10:15.833
He only booked you after seeing
"River of Time".

630
01:10:16.916 --> 01:10:19.791
Must be great for your grandkids.

631
01:10:21.583 --> 01:10:27.458
Well, it's still difficult with Daniel.
The worst part is that his kids suffer.

632
01:10:27.583 --> 01:10:31.416
They never get to come here
to see their grandpa.

633
01:10:31.541 --> 01:10:33.708
But hey...

634
01:10:33.833 --> 01:10:38.166
-This script...
-Not that bad, huh?

635
01:10:38.291 --> 01:10:40.916
-It's stunning.
-So you're in?

636
01:10:41.041 --> 01:10:43.624
Yeah, I have to do this.

637
01:10:43.749 --> 01:10:46.499
Come on, let's grab a drink.

638
01:10:58.333 --> 01:11:00.416
No. Oh.

639
01:11:01.916 --> 01:11:05.916
I don't think there's much
hand-held camera in this.

640
01:11:06.041 --> 01:11:09.541
I'm thinking dolly and tripod.

641
01:11:17.791 --> 01:11:22.124
Sorry for not staying in touch.
I'm bad at that between films.

642
01:11:22.249 --> 01:11:24.749
Same here, you know. Same here.

643
01:11:30.124 --> 01:11:34.249
I have to... I'll let you know
if this works out.

644
01:11:36.916 --> 01:11:40.208
Netflix also wants a say.

645
01:11:41.833 --> 01:11:45.166
They want us to talk to others as well.

646
01:11:51.208 --> 01:11:53.541
Right, so that's how it is?

647
01:11:55.416 --> 01:11:57.624
Yeah.

648
01:11:58.166 --> 01:12:00.249
That's how it is.

649
01:12:19.333 --> 01:12:21.291
Cheers.

650
01:12:22.291 --> 01:12:24.374
Good to see you.

651
01:13:12.124 --> 01:13:15.833
Hi, Nora.
I just wanted to call you to say that...

652
01:13:21.666 --> 01:13:24.249
We have to talk to each other.

653
01:13:27.166 --> 01:13:30.374
It can't be the case that...

654
01:13:32.124 --> 01:13:35.666
I don't like going to the theatre,
for example.

655
01:13:35.791 --> 01:13:39.083
And it's not that I don't like theatre.

656
01:13:39.208 --> 01:13:43.958
It's just that...
I can't stand watching it.

657
01:13:46.708 --> 01:13:48.833
But Chekhov is...

658
01:13:48.958 --> 01:13:51.666
I can read Chekhov.

659
01:13:51.791 --> 01:13:54.458
It's really...

660
01:13:54.583 --> 01:13:57.958
Not the plays, but...

661
01:14:00.166 --> 01:14:03.499
I'm sensitive. So are you.

662
01:14:03.624 --> 01:14:07.041
We're... alike in that sense.

663
01:14:07.666 --> 01:14:09.749
What the fuck.

664
01:15:09.083 --> 01:15:10.749
Oh, thanks.

665
01:15:10.874 --> 01:15:12.874
I was wondering...

666
01:15:12.999 --> 01:15:15.083
Who is the "you"?

667
01:15:15.208 --> 01:15:19.083
She says "you know".
Who is the you?

668
01:15:20.999 --> 01:15:23.083
Who do you think?

669
01:15:31.083 --> 01:15:36.041
I don't think it's her mother.
She'd never say that to her.

670
01:15:39.541 --> 01:15:43.791
It feels like it's just an inner voice,
I guess.

671
01:15:44.541 --> 01:15:49.083
It just feels weird to speak
and not be talking to anyone.

672
01:15:50.416 --> 01:15:52.666
Great.

673
01:15:52.791 --> 01:15:56.958
-Let's do it once with Ingrid listening.
-I am.

674
01:15:59.249 --> 01:16:02.374
Come here. Try it sitting here.

675
01:16:02.499 --> 01:16:05.458
-Just to see.
-Okay.

676
01:16:05.583 --> 01:16:08.416
And you pick it up at...

677
01:16:10.458 --> 01:16:13.374
"I had this kind of crisis."
Talk to Ingrid.

678
01:16:13.499 --> 01:16:18.458
If you want to, you can touch her.
Feel that you're her mother.

679
01:16:20.833 --> 01:16:24.666
And remember, she's never
told anyone about this.

680
01:16:24.791 --> 01:16:28.124
She's embarrassed about praying like this.

681
01:16:28.249 --> 01:16:31.916
May try to hide it or laugh
at yourself, but...

682
01:16:33.291 --> 01:16:35.374
You're ashamed.

683
01:16:37.749 --> 01:16:40.958
You're ashamed of being weak.

684
01:16:42.041 --> 01:16:44.124
Okay.

685
01:16:49.624 --> 01:16:51.374
Okay.

686
01:16:56.833 --> 01:17:00.083
"I had this kind of crisis."

687
01:17:01.666 --> 01:17:04.333
"I was alone in the house again,"

688
01:17:04.458 --> 01:17:07.166
"lying in my bed, crying."

689
01:17:10.708 --> 01:17:14.083
"I know everyone lies in bed,
crying, but..."

690
01:17:20.166 --> 01:17:24.166
"Someone said praying
isn't really talking to God."

691
01:17:25.458 --> 01:17:28.749
"It's acknowledging the despair."

692
01:17:28.874 --> 01:17:33.583
"To throw yourself on the ground
because that's all you can do."

693
01:17:35.833 --> 01:17:39.833
"Not unlike lying with your heart broken,
thinking..."

694
01:17:41.083 --> 01:17:44.208
"'Please call me. Please forgive me.'"

695
01:17:44.333 --> 01:17:46.458
"'Please take me back.'"

696
01:17:51.166 --> 01:17:53.249
"And..."

697
01:17:53.374 --> 01:17:55.874
"I had fucked up."

698
01:17:57.916 --> 01:18:00.708
"And I was alone,"

699
01:18:01.833 --> 01:18:03.916
"and crying."

700
01:18:05.416 --> 01:18:08.249
"And then, for the first time,"

701
01:18:11.874 --> 01:18:13.958
"I sat down on the floor"

702
01:18:15.374 --> 01:18:16.916
"and prayed."

703
01:18:19.624 --> 01:18:23.541
"I don't know who I said it to,
but I said it out loud."

704
01:18:26.416 --> 01:18:29.374
"'Help me.'"

705
01:18:29.499 --> 01:18:32.624
"'I can't do this anymore.'"

706
01:18:32.749 --> 01:18:35.166
"'I can't do it alone.'"

707
01:18:36.916 --> 01:18:39.124
"'I want a home.'"

708
01:18:39.249 --> 01:18:41.333
"'I want a home.'"

709
01:18:58.333 --> 01:19:00.958
I wish her... I wish

710
01:19:01.083 --> 01:19:04.416
her mother was like this in the film.

711
01:19:06.666 --> 01:19:08.749
Exactly.

712
01:19:19.666 --> 01:19:23.791
-Are you sure doing this in English works?
-Yeah, of course.

713
01:19:24.333 --> 01:19:28.541
Maybe I should have a Norwegian accent,
like Ingrid?

714
01:19:28.666 --> 01:19:30.958
I don't have an accent. Do I?

715
01:19:31.083 --> 01:19:32.999
-She's Swedish.
-Do I?

716
01:19:33.124 --> 01:19:37.958
Your accent's non-existent.
I just mean... Wouldn't it be weird

717
01:19:38.083 --> 01:19:42.208
if everyone has a Scandinavian accent,
and I sound like this?

718
01:19:42.333 --> 01:19:44.666
Maybe. I'll think about it.

719
01:19:48.499 --> 01:19:52.791
Could I ask a little bit more
about the character?

720
01:19:52.916 --> 01:19:56.083
I think my big question is still "Why?"

721
01:19:56.208 --> 01:19:59.458
Why she decides to kill herself.

722
01:19:59.583 --> 01:20:02.833
Yeah.
So what do you think?

723
01:20:06.999 --> 01:20:10.416
I get that she's depressed,
like her mother.

724
01:20:11.958 --> 01:20:13.916
But she has a child.

725
01:20:14.999 --> 01:20:18.374
Well, that's the question
the film is asking, isn't it?

726
01:20:18.499 --> 01:20:22.208
I think you have to find your own reason.

727
01:20:27.374 --> 01:20:30.374
Could I ask about your mother?

728
01:20:32.624 --> 01:20:36.708
I don't mean to pry, I just feel
she might be relevant.

729
01:20:37.333 --> 01:20:40.083
Well, this is not about my mother.

730
01:22:01.499 --> 01:22:06.041
This is her State Police registry card
and her prison camp file.

731
01:22:06.999 --> 01:22:12.583
Karin Irgens' witness statement is here.
You applied for access to the whole file.

732
01:22:12.708 --> 01:22:18.749
And the file contains photographs from
reconstructions of the torture methods.

733
01:22:18.874 --> 01:22:23.416
Well, the images are unsettling.
I thought you should know.

734
01:22:23.541 --> 01:22:27.791
But I've marked the most relevant
information with tags.

735
01:22:27.916 --> 01:22:29.874
Well, thank you.

736
01:22:49.999 --> 01:22:52.083
IRGENS, Karin

737
01:22:52.208 --> 01:22:55.874
Sentenced for: Anti-nazi propaganda

738
01:23:33.833 --> 01:23:36.166
Treason trial

739
01:23:36.291 --> 01:23:40.499
Document list
- regarding instances of torture

740
01:23:52.499 --> 01:23:58.041
Re: Defendant's crimes of treason -
the torture of Karin Irgens

741
01:24:08.458 --> 01:24:11.999
They tightened the leg clamps
until I screamed.

742
01:24:12.124 --> 01:24:15.624
The interrogator tied me to a stove.
It was very hot.

743
01:24:15.749 --> 01:24:18.583
They beat me with a rod.

744
01:24:18.708 --> 01:24:21.749
It was most painful.
The pain endured for months.

745
01:25:01.958 --> 01:25:08.666
<i>When she told Even about it later,</i>
<i>it didn't feel as if she had much to add.</i>

746
01:25:08.791 --> 01:25:11.208
<i>They already knew most of it.</i>

747
01:25:13.416 --> 01:25:17.791
<i>It was hard to say why it had made</i>
<i>such a strong impression.</i>

748
01:25:17.916 --> 01:25:21.791
<i>Was it her grandmother's</i>
<i>witness statement, the dry,</i>

749
01:25:21.916 --> 01:25:24.624
<i>unsentimental way she described it?</i>

750
01:25:24.749 --> 01:25:31.499
<i>Or was it because she was just one of many</i>
<i>victims who testified in the same file?</i>

751
01:25:32.041 --> 01:25:37.791
<i>The police report on her suicide</i>
<i>15 years later was thin.</i>

752
01:25:37.916 --> 01:25:41.916
<i>There were no photographs.</i>
<i>But her father's name was there.</i>

753
01:25:42.041 --> 01:25:44.458
<i>He hadn't been questioned.</i>

754
01:25:44.583 --> 01:25:48.499
<i>He was just seven years old at the time.</i>

755
01:25:58.124 --> 01:26:00.916
Then you pan quickly as hell.

756
01:26:01.041 --> 01:26:03.333
No, wait.

757
01:26:03.458 --> 01:26:05.666
Pay attention now.

758
01:26:14.041 --> 01:26:16.208
Cut.

759
01:26:16.333 --> 01:26:17.916
Show me.

760
01:26:18.499 --> 01:26:20.583
Good, huh?

761
01:26:27.124 --> 01:26:29.124
May I see?

762
01:26:38.624 --> 01:26:39.833
Great!

763
01:26:44.958 --> 01:26:48.041
Why don't we go to Nora's premiere
together?

764
01:26:48.166 --> 01:26:51.583
We could grab some dinner here first.

765
01:26:51.708 --> 01:26:55.541
I think I have to take a cab
straight from the office.

766
01:26:55.666 --> 01:26:58.499
-But you're coming?
-Of course.

767
01:27:08.166 --> 01:27:10.958
I want Erik to be in my film.

768
01:27:12.749 --> 01:27:15.249
-He's perfect.
-Okay?

769
01:27:16.833 --> 01:27:21.041
It's not that many lines,
but it's a key part, Rachel's son.

770
01:27:24.291 --> 01:27:26.916
That's nice, but...

771
01:27:27.041 --> 01:27:30.916
I don't know, he's not exactly
the actor type.

772
01:27:31.041 --> 01:27:33.749
I don't want an actor type.

773
01:27:34.291 --> 01:27:38.791
There's nothing worse than children
with jazz hands in a film.

774
01:27:38.916 --> 01:27:42.416
Like they're in a musical or something.

775
01:27:42.541 --> 01:27:45.041
There's something about Erik.

776
01:27:46.124 --> 01:27:48.791
He has secrets.

777
01:27:52.666 --> 01:27:56.291
You were also a bit shy, right?

778
01:27:56.416 --> 01:27:58.541
But great on screen.

779
01:27:58.666 --> 01:28:01.166
You should have kept at it.

780
01:28:09.249 --> 01:28:14.124
<i>-"I want three glasses of juice."</i>
-"I want three glasses of juice."

781
01:28:15.208 --> 01:28:19.708
<i>-"Thank you for washing my clothes."</i>
-"Thank you for washing my clothes."

782
01:28:19.833 --> 01:28:24.583
<i>-"Thank you for washing my clothes."</i>
-"Thank you for washing my clothes."

783
01:28:47.999 --> 01:28:51.208
"I almost don't dare to close my eyes."

784
01:28:51.333 --> 01:28:55.749
"I'm afraid that when I open them,
he won't be here anymore."

785
01:28:57.874 --> 01:28:59.791
Let's take a break.

786
01:29:09.749 --> 01:29:12.208
You seem a little distracted.

787
01:29:14.333 --> 01:29:16.208
Just... A bit tired.

788
01:29:17.833 --> 01:29:20.249
You can't go wrong here.

789
01:29:21.541 --> 01:29:25.291
The only thing you can do wrong
is not to listen.

790
01:29:28.083 --> 01:29:30.166
And don't rush it.

791
01:29:35.333 --> 01:29:37.124
Okay?

792
01:29:37.249 --> 01:29:39.916
Let's try one without the accent.

793
01:29:45.124 --> 01:29:48.999
We'll take it from... from...

794
01:29:49.124 --> 01:29:51.999
Previous page, on the top.

795
01:29:58.374 --> 01:30:02.833
-Hey. It's so nice to meet you.
-Nice to meet you.

796
01:30:02.958 --> 01:30:07.708
-Sorry you had to wait.
-No, thank you for taking the time.

797
01:30:22.208 --> 01:30:24.416
Why didn't you want the role?

798
01:30:30.499 --> 01:30:32.583
I can't work with him.

799
01:30:33.749 --> 01:30:35.833
Why?

800
01:30:39.333 --> 01:30:41.666
We can't really talk.

801
01:30:41.791 --> 01:30:44.541
-But he wanted you to do it.
-Yeah.

802
01:30:45.874 --> 01:30:48.416
Yeah.

803
01:30:48.541 --> 01:30:51.333
I don't know.

804
01:30:53.874 --> 01:30:56.541
Just keep thinking that he...

805
01:30:57.708 --> 01:30:59.791
He made a mistake.

806
01:31:04.083 --> 01:31:06.166
I just can't...

807
01:31:08.916 --> 01:31:12.041
I can't get a handle on her.

808
01:31:12.916 --> 01:31:18.166
The more that I study her,
the more lost I feel trying to be her.

809
01:31:18.291 --> 01:31:21.166
It's like her sadness is...

810
01:31:21.291 --> 01:31:24.291
It's such an overwhelming part of her.

811
01:31:24.416 --> 01:31:26.749
It's a beautiful theme.

812
01:31:28.499 --> 01:31:31.166
But I can't tell if that's just

813
01:31:31.291 --> 01:31:34.874
the cause of everything.

814
01:31:34.999 --> 01:31:37.166
Or is it...

815
01:31:37.291 --> 01:31:40.541
I don't know.
A symptom of something deeper?

816
01:31:48.249 --> 01:31:51.499
I don't wanna disappoint him, you know.

817
01:32:02.374 --> 01:32:04.458
Well, he's a...

818
01:32:06.999 --> 01:32:09.833
Very difficult person.

819
01:32:09.958 --> 01:32:13.999
But he's a really good director,
and he sees something in you.

820
01:32:14.124 --> 01:32:16.541
You'd be stupid to walk away.

821
01:32:24.458 --> 01:32:28.291
I'm not used to this.
I'm used to being in control.

822
01:32:29.708 --> 01:32:33.083
And if I wasn't in control,
I would enjoy it.

823
01:32:33.624 --> 01:32:35.708
Get it?

824
01:32:38.249 --> 01:32:40.833
Now I'm too old.

825
01:32:40.958 --> 01:32:45.541
There's barbed wire on the prairie.
The last damn Mohican.

826
01:32:45.666 --> 01:32:49.416
And the new guy keeps
sending me moodboards.

827
01:32:51.083 --> 01:32:53.624
Jeez, I really miss Peter.

828
01:32:53.749 --> 01:32:57.208
When did he become so goddamn frail?

829
01:33:02.166 --> 01:33:04.374
I'm too tired, Michael.

830
01:33:04.499 --> 01:33:08.124
What the hell happened?
15 years ago, I was 55.

831
01:33:12.374 --> 01:33:14.874
Maybe you're right.

832
01:33:17.499 --> 01:33:19.583
Maybe we are too old.

833
01:33:22.916 --> 01:33:25.749
This might be our last film together.

834
01:33:27.916 --> 01:33:32.624
Just don't tell me you need to
keep up with the times or anything.

835
01:33:33.708 --> 01:33:35.624
Promise me that.

836
01:33:36.624 --> 01:33:38.583
This theme...

837
01:33:39.666 --> 01:33:42.499
We know you've touched on it earlier.

838
01:33:43.583 --> 01:33:48.958
But you've never confronted it
in this way before.

839
01:33:49.083 --> 01:33:51.541
So purely. So personally.

840
01:33:52.624 --> 01:33:56.333
Okay, so it took all those years

841
01:33:56.458 --> 01:33:59.208
to get here.

842
01:33:59.333 --> 01:34:02.458
But you're gonna make this film, dammit.

843
01:34:03.541 --> 01:34:05.791
And do it your way.

844
01:34:07.374 --> 01:34:08.666
Got it?

845
01:34:51.999 --> 01:34:54.083
-Yeah.
-We'll just...

846
01:34:55.999 --> 01:34:57.916
Okay.

847
01:34:59.958 --> 01:35:01.583
Wait.

848
01:35:04.083 --> 01:35:06.874
Could you loosen it a bit?

849
01:35:09.541 --> 01:35:11.291
There.

850
01:36:50.374 --> 01:36:54.083
-Hi!
-Hi! Congratulations. You were fantastic.

851
01:36:54.208 --> 01:36:57.458
-Really great.
-Thanks.

852
01:36:57.583 --> 01:37:00.583
Everyone thinks it's so exciting.

853
01:37:01.374 --> 01:37:04.208
We got through it, I guess.

854
01:37:04.333 --> 01:37:06.958
Well, it was fun. Yeah.

855
01:37:11.958 --> 01:37:16.124
-Are you okay?
-Yeah, I'm fine.

856
01:37:16.249 --> 01:37:20.666
-You haven't answered my calls lately.
-I know.

857
01:37:21.208 --> 01:37:24.874
-Did you come with dad?
-No, he couldn't make it.

858
01:37:27.583 --> 01:37:33.124
We've got a babysitter, so we're going for
a drink at Tekehtopa. Wanna join us?

859
01:37:33.249 --> 01:37:36.666
Excuse me?
I really must congratulate you.

860
01:37:36.791 --> 01:37:41.833
-It was simply a fantastic performance.
-Thank you so much.

861
01:38:13.791 --> 01:38:17.541
Yes, I'm heading there now.
I'll let you know.

862
01:38:17.666 --> 01:38:20.624
But I can't this evening.

863
01:38:21.249 --> 01:38:24.083
I've often gone on stage sick.

864
01:38:24.208 --> 01:38:29.458
I've never cancelled a performance before,
but it's simply not possible.

865
01:38:29.583 --> 01:38:31.958
I'm so sorry.

866
01:38:32.083 --> 01:38:34.583
Yeah, thank you. Thanks.

867
01:38:34.708 --> 01:38:37.249
Okay. Bye.

868
01:39:17.874 --> 01:39:21.124
-Where's Erik?
-He'll be here soon.

869
01:39:23.958 --> 01:39:26.624
Why didn't you come to Nora's premiere?

870
01:39:26.749 --> 01:39:28.833
She didn't want me there.

871
01:39:32.666 --> 01:39:35.291
Let's see... This is the original.

872
01:39:35.416 --> 01:39:37.874
And here's the translation,

873
01:39:37.999 --> 01:39:43.583
so Erik can practice his English lines.
Don't worry about his accent.

874
01:39:43.708 --> 01:39:45.791
Have you talked to Michael?

875
01:39:45.916 --> 01:39:48.749
He has to take leave from school.

876
01:39:51.624 --> 01:39:53.999
I'm not sure it's a good idea.

877
01:39:54.124 --> 01:39:57.624
It's a great idea! It'll be awesome.

878
01:39:57.749 --> 01:40:01.041
-No, I'm not sure he wants to.
-Sure he does.

879
01:40:01.166 --> 01:40:03.583
-How do you know?
-I've talked to him.

880
01:40:03.708 --> 01:40:07.083
-Have you talked to him?
-I mentioned it.

881
01:40:07.208 --> 01:40:11.458
-He was ecstatic.
-You can't talk to him without me there!

882
01:40:18.958 --> 01:40:21.458
When you played in my film...

883
01:40:22.583 --> 01:40:25.833
It's the most beautiful memory I've got.

884
01:40:26.916 --> 01:40:30.958
This will be good.
Let's just look at the scenes.

885
01:40:31.083 --> 01:40:34.416
I think Rachel will enjoy
filming them with him.

886
01:40:34.541 --> 01:40:39.208
There's happiness in them.
She needs to see other aspects of...

887
01:40:39.333 --> 01:40:41.416
Stop talking. Just stop.

888
01:40:42.499 --> 01:40:45.708
-I don't want Erik to be in the film.
-But Agnes...

889
01:40:45.833 --> 01:40:48.374
And yes, it was nice, dad.

890
01:40:48.499 --> 01:40:53.333
It was fantastic. The time of my life.
I got to be with you.

891
01:40:53.458 --> 01:40:56.333
I was the centre of the universe.

892
01:40:56.458 --> 01:41:01.124
-Then you left. I didn't see you for ages.
-But it was fantastic!

893
01:41:02.208 --> 01:41:04.249
Yes.

894
01:41:04.374 --> 01:41:06.374
But I don't understand.

895
01:41:06.499 --> 01:41:11.874
I don't see how you can switch from being
so close and real to just vanishing.

896
01:41:11.999 --> 01:41:16.416
Suddenly someone else is
the centre of the universe.

897
01:41:16.541 --> 01:41:18.999
-Just read it!
-Jesus Christ!

898
01:41:19.124 --> 01:41:22.958
I don't care!
I don't care whether it's good or not.

899
01:41:23.083 --> 01:41:24.999
I care about Erik.

900
01:41:31.083 --> 01:41:33.083
Hi!

901
01:41:34.166 --> 01:41:36.624
Hi!

902
01:41:36.749 --> 01:41:39.833
-Grandpa was just leaving.
-Oh.

903
01:41:41.874 --> 01:41:44.874
Hi. How have you been?

904
01:41:44.999 --> 01:41:48.916
-Okay.
-Good to see you. I've missed you today.

905
01:41:49.041 --> 01:41:51.208
-Me too.
-You have?

906
01:41:54.416 --> 01:41:58.166
-So it's a good thing I came now.
-Right?

907
01:41:58.291 --> 01:42:02.708
Very good.
What shall we do while we wait for dad?

908
01:43:40.708 --> 01:43:42.791
Hi.

909
01:43:46.208 --> 01:43:48.916
To what do I owe the honour?

910
01:43:51.583 --> 01:43:55.374
-Are you okay?
-Yeah, I guess.

911
01:43:57.041 --> 01:43:59.708
Come on in. I'll make a cup of tea.

912
01:44:16.083 --> 01:44:18.416
-There you go.
-Thank you.

913
01:44:24.041 --> 01:44:27.874
So you don't wanna do the film anymore.

914
01:44:31.624 --> 01:44:33.708
I, uh...

915
01:44:34.833 --> 01:44:36.916
I just...

916
01:44:39.499 --> 01:44:42.166
I just don't wanna let you down.

917
01:44:45.374 --> 01:44:48.999
I won't drop out if
you really want me to stay.

918
01:44:52.708 --> 01:44:55.916
Is it just me?
This just doesn't feel right.

919
01:44:56.999 --> 01:45:01.041
It doesn't feel right for you either,
does it? Me doing this film.

920
01:45:01.166 --> 01:45:03.291
Of course it does.

921
01:45:09.083 --> 01:45:11.124
I don't think it does.

922
01:45:12.249 --> 01:45:16.124
You know, you asked me to dye my hair

923
01:45:16.249 --> 01:45:18.999
the same colour as your daughter.

924
01:45:20.458 --> 01:45:24.499
And I mean, you don't want
this film to be in English, do you?

925
01:45:24.624 --> 01:45:27.708
That feels wrong to you too. Right?

926
01:45:31.874 --> 01:45:34.708
And I don't wanna disappoint you.

927
01:45:40.124 --> 01:45:42.208
I'm sorry.

928
01:45:48.916 --> 01:45:52.666
I let you down, Rachel. I'm sorry.

929
01:45:56.124 --> 01:46:00.583
Most actors would do the role
even if it felt wrong.

930
01:46:00.708 --> 01:46:04.541
Or just leave,
and let the agents sort it out.

931
01:46:07.583 --> 01:46:10.166
But you came here.

932
01:46:13.749 --> 01:46:15.833
You're a good person.

933
01:46:26.208 --> 01:46:28.291
Come on.

934
01:46:44.208 --> 01:46:46.999
Please don't look at this as a failure.

935
01:46:48.208 --> 01:46:52.041
-You're a great actress. I meant it.
-Thank you.

936
01:48:55.708 --> 01:48:57.791
-Hi.
-Hi.

937
01:48:59.583 --> 01:49:02.833
Sorry for showing up like this,
but why don't you answer my calls?

938
01:49:02.958 --> 01:49:05.041
I'm sorry.

939
01:49:13.624 --> 01:49:15.708
-Are you okay?
-Mm.

940
01:49:20.124 --> 01:49:22.749
You have to answer the phone.

941
01:49:22.874 --> 01:49:26.291
You know how anxious I get when you don't.

942
01:49:30.041 --> 01:49:32.583
What's going on? Are you sick?

943
01:49:33.124 --> 01:49:35.208
No.

944
01:50:03.541 --> 01:50:06.499
I had a row with dad yesterday.

945
01:50:09.208 --> 01:50:12.583
I was furious.
He wants to put Erik in the film.

946
01:50:12.708 --> 01:50:14.874
I don't want that.

947
01:50:16.416 --> 01:50:18.833
But then I read the script.

948
01:50:23.666 --> 01:50:27.499
And it was so great.
Not at all what I imagined.

949
01:50:32.791 --> 01:50:37.291
Right. I know you don't want
anything to do with it.

950
01:50:37.416 --> 01:50:40.249
But I still want you to read it.

951
01:50:44.874 --> 01:50:47.333
Because I sense it's about you.

952
01:50:47.874 --> 01:50:49.624
Or...

953
01:50:50.708 --> 01:50:54.541
I think he wrote it for you.
It's not about his mother.

954
01:51:00.249 --> 01:51:02.333
Well.

955
01:51:29.458 --> 01:51:32.958
Could you read this bit for me?

956
01:51:34.041 --> 01:51:36.916
Don't mind that it's like...

957
01:51:37.041 --> 01:51:39.749
Well, a bit overwritten.

958
01:51:39.874 --> 01:51:41.791
Just read it.

959
01:51:50.749 --> 01:51:54.374
"You know, I don't believe in God at all."

960
01:51:54.499 --> 01:51:58.499
"We came from a home
where all that was irrelevant."

961
01:52:01.541 --> 01:52:07.874
"We weren't baptised. My sister and I had
a civil confirmation just for the money."

962
01:52:07.999 --> 01:52:10.791
"Then I had this kind of crisis."

963
01:52:10.916 --> 01:52:14.916
"I was alone in the house again,
lying in bed, crying."

964
01:52:15.041 --> 01:52:19.958
"I know everyone lies in bed crying
at some point, but..."

965
01:52:20.458 --> 01:52:24.374
"Someone said praying
isn't really talking to God."

966
01:52:24.499 --> 01:52:27.249
"It's acknowledging despair."

967
01:52:29.124 --> 01:52:33.874
"To throw yourself on the ground
because that's all you can do."

968
01:52:33.999 --> 01:52:36.333
"Not unlike heartbreak:"

969
01:52:36.458 --> 01:52:39.958
"'Call me. Please change your mind.'"

970
01:52:40.374 --> 01:52:42.458
"'Take me back.'"

971
01:52:43.916 --> 01:52:46.583
"There I was. I'd ruined everything."

972
01:52:46.708 --> 01:52:49.874
"I was alone, lying there, crying."

973
01:52:49.999 --> 01:52:53.166
"And then, for the first time,"

974
01:52:53.291 --> 01:52:56.874
"I sat down and prayed."

975
01:52:56.999 --> 01:52:59.291
"It's hard to explain."

976
01:52:59.416 --> 01:53:04.208
"I don't know who I prayed to,
but I said it out loud."

977
01:53:04.333 --> 01:53:06.416
"'Help me.'"

978
01:53:08.708 --> 01:53:11.749
"'I can't do this.'"

979
01:53:12.833 --> 01:53:14.916
"'I can't do it alone.'"

980
01:53:16.416 --> 01:53:18.499
"'I want a home.'"

981
01:53:22.791 --> 01:53:25.291
"'I want a home.'"

982
01:54:51.291 --> 01:54:54.541
Did you tell dad about...

983
01:54:56.124 --> 01:54:59.416
-My suicide attempt?
-No, of course not.

984
01:55:03.624 --> 01:55:07.374
I thought the same thing.
"How does he know?"

985
01:55:12.291 --> 01:55:16.166
It's mixed up with a lot of things
about his mother.

986
01:55:16.291 --> 01:55:21.416
But in some scenes it's as if he was there
when you went through it.

987
01:55:21.541 --> 01:55:23.458
Well, he wasn't.

988
01:55:24.958 --> 01:55:27.041
You were.

989
01:55:47.666 --> 01:55:49.666
How did it happen?

990
01:55:50.666 --> 01:55:54.458
You turned out fine,
and I became fucked up.

991
01:55:54.583 --> 01:55:57.041
That's not true.

992
01:55:59.541 --> 01:56:02.749
Why didn't our childhood ruin you?

993
01:56:02.874 --> 01:56:05.624
It hasn't always been easy for me.

994
01:56:07.416 --> 01:56:10.166
But you've managed to make a family.

995
01:56:12.166 --> 01:56:15.208
-A home.
-Yeah.

996
01:56:18.583 --> 01:56:22.749
There's one major difference
in the way we grew up.

997
01:56:22.874 --> 01:56:24.958
I had you.

998
01:56:28.249 --> 01:56:33.999
I know you think you're incapable
of caring, but you were there for me.

999
01:56:35.541 --> 01:56:37.624
When mom was down.

1000
01:56:40.499 --> 01:56:42.583
You washed my hair.

1001
01:56:49.499 --> 01:56:51.583
Combed it.

1002
01:56:52.583 --> 01:56:54.666
Got me to school.

1003
01:56:57.583 --> 01:56:59.791
I felt safe.

1004
01:57:28.833 --> 01:57:30.916
I love you.

1005
01:57:34.708 --> 01:57:36.541
You too.

1006
01:57:36.666 --> 01:57:38.749
I mean, I love you too.

1007
01:58:56.583 --> 01:58:59.083
Does it feel okay?

1008
01:59:00.166 --> 01:59:03.583
How wonderful to wake up to such beauty.

1009
01:59:04.958 --> 01:59:10.208
You have a wonderful smile.
Does your boyfriend say so? He should.

1010
01:59:10.333 --> 01:59:14.374
Well, he does, actually.
I've heard it before.

1011
01:59:15.458 --> 01:59:19.333
Jeez! Do you have anything else?
Like champagne?

1012
01:59:19.458 --> 01:59:21.708
Champagne?

1013
02:00:29.749 --> 02:00:32.166
Can I have some cookies too?

1014
02:00:33.999 --> 02:00:38.291
-You know...
-Omar and the others always get cookies.

1015
02:00:38.416 --> 02:00:42.791
-With chocolate.
-Just finish your sandwiches first.

1016
02:00:58.749 --> 02:01:00.833
Okay.

1017
02:01:15.333 --> 02:01:17.416
-Bye then.
-Bye.

1018
02:03:00.249 --> 02:03:03.583
-Hi. What's the matter?
-I forgot my mobile.

1019
02:03:13.999 --> 02:03:16.083
-Bye.
-Goodbye.

1020
02:03:18.583 --> 02:03:20.666
Okay.

1021
02:04:31.541 --> 02:04:33.249
Cut.

1022
02:04:35.041 --> 02:04:38.458
-Perfect.
-We've cut. Moving on.
